dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, ms. thompson, how are you?
[patient] i'm doing well, thank you.
[doctor] good. so i know the nurse told you about dax. i'd like to tell dax a little bit about you.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] so i see that you're a 27 year old female with a history of carpal tunnel syndrome, had a surgery on that a couple years ago. you've had chronic back pain, and you've had depression. and you're here today for some knee pain. is that correct?
[patient] yeah, that's correct.
[doctor] okay. and can you tell me a little bit more about the knee pain?
[patient] so over the course of the month, i've been swimming more regularly and trying to be more active, and i've started noticing my right knee pain.
[doctor] okay.
[patient] and i'm like, i can swim, you know, i can do like ten laps no problem. but now, after like, a couple of laps, it just starts hurting.
[doctor] okay. and you didn't have any injury or trauma to the knee?
[patient] no, nothing like that.
[doctor] okay. and you said this started about a month ago?
[patient] yeah, over the course of the month.
[doctor] okay. and is the pain constant or does it come and go?
[patient] it comes and goes.
[doctor] okay. and is it just the right knee or is it both?
[patient] just the right knee.
[doctor] okay. and is there any swelling or bruising?
[patient] there's no bruising.
[doctor] okay.
[patient] there's just like a little bit of redness.
[doctor] okay. and what about any numbness or tingling?
[patient] no, nothing like that.
[doctor] okay. and have you tried any treatments or medications for it?
[patient] i've taken some ibuprofen, and it does help a little bit.
[doctor] okay. and any other treatments, like ice or heat?
[patient] i've tried ice, and it does help a little bit.
[doctor] okay. and is the pain worse with activity or is it just there all the time?
[patient] it's worse with activity.
[doctor] okay. and do you remember if it hurt when i pressed on it?
[patient] yeah, it did.
[doctor] okay. and what about on the other side?
[patient] no, not on the other side.
[doctor] okay. and is the pain just in the front or is it all around?
[patient] it's just in the front.
[doctor] okay. and any pain when i move your leg like this?
[patient] yeah, a little bit.
[doctor] okay. and how about when i move it like this?
[patient] yeah, that hurts a little bit too.
[doctor] okay. and do you have any pain when you bear weight on it?
[patient] yeah, yeah, i do.
[doctor] okay. and do you have any pain when you do squats or lunges?
[patient] yeah, yeah, those are really bad.
[doctor] okay. and any pain when you climb stairs?
[patient] yeah, yeah, those are pretty bad too.
[doctor] okay. and have you noticed that you're more tired when you're walking or doing things?
[patient] yeah, yeah, i am.
[doctor] okay. and have you noticed any swelling in your legs?
[patient] no, not really.
[doctor] okay. and any numbness or tingling in your legs?
[patient] no, not really.
[doctor] okay. and do you have any shortness of breath or chest pain?
[patient] no, nothing like that.
[doctor] okay. and any palpitations or your heart racing?
[patient] no, not at all.
[doctor] okay. and any dizziness or headaches?
[patient] no, nothing like that.
[doctor] okay. and any nausea or vomiting?
[patient] no, nothing like that.
[doctor] okay. and any abdominal pain?
[patient] no, nothing like that.
[doctor] okay. and any back pain?
[patient] i do have back pain, but it's been doing really well.
[doctor] okay. and any pain radiating down your legs?
[patient] no, nothing like that.
[doctor] okay. and how's your depression been?
[patient] it's been going really well. i'm doing a lot better.
[doctor] okay. and are you taking your medications?
[patient] yeah, i am.
[doctor] okay. and are you seeing your therapist?
[patient] yeah, i am.
[doctor] okay. and are you getting outside?
[patient] yeah, i'm trying to get outside more.
[doctor] okay. and how's your carpal tunnel?
[patient] it's doing really well. i get full range of motion and it feels a lot better.
[doctor] okay. and are you having any problems with that?
[patient] no, not at all.
[doctor] okay. great. well, let's go ahead and do a physical exam. i'm gon na be calling out some of my findings. if you have any questions, please let me know.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] so your vital signs are all within normal limits. your heart sounds are normal, your lungs are clear. on your neck exam, you don't have any thyroid enlargement. your back is supple. on your musculoskeletal exam, you do have some pain with patellar inhibition and some pain with range of motion of the right knee. otherwise, you don't have any lower extremity edema. on your neurological exam, your grip strength is equal bilaterally, which is good. your mood and affect are appropriate. and on your respiratory exam, your lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. no wheezes, rales, or rhonchi. on your cardiovascular exam, you have a regular rate and rhythm. no murmurs, gallops, or rubs. no extra heart sounds.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and i reviewed your x-ray of your right knee. it looks unremarkable. so, let's talk a little bit about my assessment and plan for you. so, for your right knee pain, i think you have chondromalacia of the patella. you've been more active lately, but you're unaware of any injury to the knee. your recent x-rays were normal, but you did have some discomfort on exam. i'm going to prescribe you ibuprofen 600 milligrams four times a day with food. i do want you to do some straight leg raising exercises that will help strengthen your quadriceps. i also encourage you to participate in physical activity that is less straining on your joints, such as swimming, but maybe not freestyle laps. you can stop activity all together, and if your pain does not improve in a couple of weeks, you will follow up.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] for your chronic back pain, it's stable and well controlled at this time. you should continue swimming regularly once your knee is feeling better.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] for your depression, you're doing well with your current regimen. continue with that.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and for your carpal tunnel syndrome, it's relatively stable status post carpal tunnel release. continue with your current regimen with no changes at this time.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] do you have any questions for me?
[patient] no, that's it.
[doctor] okay. great. all right, so i'll have the nurse come in and get you discharged. thank you so much for coming in today.
[patient] thank you.
[doctor] you're welcome.
[patient] okay, bye.
[doctor] bye.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] bye. }}<|eot_id|>
