dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, how are you doing today?
[patient] i'm doing okay, just feeling really tired and not having a lot of energy.
[doctor] okay, well, i'm glad you came in. i see here that you had an insect bite at your last visit. how are you feeling since then?
[patient] yeah, i went hiking in the woods and got bitten by something. i've been feeling really tired and lacking a lot of energy.
[doctor] okay, and have you noticed anything else, like any joint pain or rashes?
[patient] yeah, my elbows are really swollen and sore, and i have this bullseye rash on my stomach.
[doctor] okay, and have you been able to eat much?
[patient] not really, i've been eating a lot of crackers and just bland foods because i've been feeling nauseous.
[doctor] okay, and have you had any high fevers?
[patient] no, no fevers.
[doctor] okay, let's talk a little bit about your diabetes. how have your blood sugars been?
[patient] they've been okay, sometimes a little high or low, but nothing too far out of range.
[doctor] and are you still taking metformin?
[patient] yeah, i am.
[doctor] okay, and how about your blood pressure? have you been monitoring it at home?
[patient] yeah, i have. it's been good. i'm avoiding salty foods and i'm still taking lisinopril.
[doctor] okay, good. and any abdominal pain or diarrhea?
[patient] no, none of that.
[doctor] okay, let's do a quick physical exam. i'm just going to check your abdomen first. does it hurt when i press here?
[patient] no, it doesn't hurt.
[doctor] okay, i do feel some warmth here. have you noticed that?
[patient] yeah, it feels a little warm.
[doctor] okay, let me check your elbows. i see there is swelling and pain to palpation bilaterally. and i see a small bullseye rash on your left abdominal quadrant. how long has that been there?
[patient] it showed up a few days after the bite.
[doctor] okay, and i see that your lyme titer is elevated. i'm going to order some blood work to check your white blood cell count and a complete metabolic panel. i'm also going to order a hemoglobin a1c to check your diabetes control.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] for your newly diagnosed lyme disease, i'm going to prescribe doxycycline 100 mg twice a day for three weeks. i'm also going to touch base with an infectious disease doctor to ensure nothing else needs to be done. i want to see you again next week for follow-up for this.
[patient] okay, sounds good.
[doctor] for your hypertension, we will continue you on lisinopril 10 mg a day. i'm going to order a lipid panel to check your cholesterol levels. continue monitoring your blood pressures at home.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] for your type 2 diabetes, continue with metformin. we'll review your hemoglobin a1c to see if we need to make any changes to your medication. do you have any questions?
[patient] no, i think that's it.
[doctor] okay, great. i'll see you next week. take care.
[patient] thank you, doctor. }}<|eot_id|>
