dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hey emily good to see you today so tell me what's new with you
[patient] hey doctor i'm doing pretty well i'm here for my annual follow up for my asthma and my allergies and my food allergy
[doctor] okay so i see that you were switched over to wixela because of some insurance changes that you had from your last visit so how is that going
[patient] it's going pretty well i've been cutting down on it though because i'm trying to join the military and they said i need to be able to do my fitness test without any inhalers so i've been weaning myself off it
[doctor] okay that makes sense so you're using the wixela once a day three times a week and then the other days you're just using it twice a day
[patient] yeah that's right
[doctor] and how are you feeling on that regimen
[patient] i'm feeling good i feel like i can do it
[doctor] okay and have you had to go to the er or urgent care for any respiratory issues in the past year
[patient] no i haven't had to do that
[doctor] okay and have you needed any prednisone for breathing issues in the past year
[patient] no i haven't had to do that either
[doctor] okay good so tell me about your allergies how are you handling that
[patient] well i've been using zyrtec in the spring and summer and fall and it's been working pretty well i don't have to use it all the time
[doctor] okay and do you have to use it in the summer
[patient] i don't have to use it all the time during the summer i only have to use it a few days a week when i'm working outside
[doctor] okay and how about any exposure to peanuts or tree nuts
[patient] i'm doing a good job avoiding peanuts i had a bad reaction last year when i was cleaning a rental car and they had a lot of peanuts in it i developed contact hives and i had to use my epipen i've been really careful since then
[doctor] okay and have you had any other accidental exposures to peanuts
[patient] no i've been really careful i wear long sleeves and change my gloves between each cleaning i think it's just because i've been around a lot of cars that had peanuts but i didn't touch them directly
[doctor] okay and what about tree nuts
[patient] i'm avoiding those too i did try almonds and cashews and they were fine but i'm still avoiding all of them
[doctor] okay and do you have your epipen with you
[patient] i do
[doctor] okay and do you have any other social habits that i should be aware of
[patient] i do smoke and i do vape but i'm trying to cut down i'm trying to quit before i join the military
[doctor] okay and do you want some help with that
[patient] i don't think so i think i can do it on my own
[doctor] okay well we'll give you some information in the handout but i think you can do it on your own also you mentioned that you have a nebulizer do you use that often
[patient] no i don't use it much i only used it a few times when i was a child and had an upper respiratory infection and a few times for a cold
[doctor] okay and how about any exertional testing have you started that
[patient] i have started that i'm actually hoping to be enlisted in the military in early spring of twenty twenty two
[doctor] okay that's great that gives you six to seven months to show that you can exert yourself without needing any inhalers
[patient] yeah i'm hoping so
[doctor] okay so let's go over some of your vitals and your test results so your act score is twenty one which is good your spirometry is better than it was in two thousand nineteen although i do recall that we did it during a wildfire season so it was a bit smoky so i think that might have affected it a little bit
[patient] yeah i remember that
[doctor] okay and you do have a history of asthma allergic rhinitis and food allergies to peanuts and tree nuts so let's talk about what i think we should do for you so for your asthma i think we should go back to using symbicort because it's a little better for you but we'll start you on alvesco two puffs twice a day and you can use the wixela in case the alvesco doesn't work well continue using your albuterol as needed and we'll have you come back in four months to repeat your act and spirometry testing
[patient] okay that sounds good
[doctor] and for your allergic rhinitis i think you can continue using the zyrtec ten milligrams as needed primarily in the spring and fall and sometimes in the summer and it sounds like your symptoms are under control so we'll continue that and for your anaphylactic reaction to peanuts i think you're doing a great job avoiding exposure and we'll continue to refill your epipen annually and i'll give you a new one today
[patient] okay that sounds good
[doctor] do you have any questions for me
[patient] no that's it
[doctor] okay great i'll see you in four months
[patient] thank you
[doctor] you're welcome }}<|eot_id|>
