dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hey jordan, how are you?
[patient] hey, i'm good. how are you?
[doctor] i'm good. so, jordan, i know the nurse told you about dax. i'd like to tell dax a little bit about you. so, jordan is a 49-year-old male with a long history of hypertension, but he had an acute episode of palpitations in march, and he went to the er for that, and he's here for a follow-up on that.
[patient] mm-hmm.
[doctor] so, jordan, tell me about what happened in march. what happened in march?
[patient] i went to the er because i was having some palpitations and high blood pressure. i thought it was a panic attack at first because i had a lot of anxiety and, uh, i was really worried. but then i thought about it, and i had had several cups of coffee that morning on an empty stomach, and i thought that might have been the cause.
[doctor] okay. so you think the coffee was the cause of your palpitations?
[patient] yeah, i do.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any episodes of palpitations since march?
[patient] no, not since march.
[doctor] okay. and how have you been monitoring your blood pressure at home?
[patient] i've been checking it every day, and it's been running between 146 and 155.
[doctor] okay. and are you taking any medications for your blood pressure?
[patient] yeah, my new primary care doctor put me on benicar hct, and i've been taking that for about a month.
[doctor] okay. and are you taking any other medications?
[patient] no, that's it.
[doctor] okay. and how about salt intake? are you watching that?
[patient] yes, i am. i've been really good about that.
[doctor] okay. and how about exercise? are you exercising regularly?
[patient] i do walk on my treadmill as often as i can.
[doctor] okay. great. and any family history of hypertension?
[patient] yeah, several family members.
[doctor] okay. so, i'm gon na go ahead and do a quick physical exam. i'll be calling out some of my findings. if you have any questions, feel free to ask. so, on your cardiovascular exam, your heart sounds are normal, your heart rate is normal, regular rhythm, normal heart sounds, and normal pulses. your breath sounds are normal. your abdomen is non-tender. there is no edema. so, let's talk a little bit about my assessment and plan. so, jordan's blood pressure has been resistant to control. he's on three medications now. i think we need to add spironolactone to his regimen. we will need to monitor his potassium and sodium, and we'll schedule a follow-up in two weeks to complete a basic metabolic panel and check his blood pressure. if we're not able to control his blood pressure with spironolactone, the next step would be to send him to the resistant hypertension clinic. we'll consider adding carvedilol next. i'll try to avoid that, given the potential for issues with erectile dysfunction, but we'll have to see how things go. so, i'm gon na go ahead and order spironolactone and i'll talk to you about the side effects, including gynecomastia and electrolyte disturbances. we'll also need to watch for signs of dehydration, such as dizziness. and i'm gon na give you some materials for the american heart association website, heart.org, to help you with tips on lowering your salt intake and increasing your potassium intake. your maximum salt intake should be 1500 to less than 2000 milligrams per day. i'll also give you materials to follow the dash diet and encourage you to meet 30 minutes of walking most days. any questions?
[patient] no, i think that's it.
[doctor] okay, great. we'll see you in two weeks for the follow-up.
[patient] sounds good. thank you. }}<|eot_id|>
