dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi nelson. so nelson, how are you?
[patient] i'm okay. how are you?
[doctor] i'm good. so i know the nurse told you about dax. i'd like to tell dax a little bit about you. so nelson is a 72-year-old male here for ongoing management of his rheumatoid arthritis. so nelson, you know, i saw you last on march fourth. how have you been doing since then?
[patient] well, i've been doing okay. i've been having some pain in my right knee and it swelled up a little bit.
[doctor] okay. and so on a scale of one to ten, how would you say your knee is feeling?
[patient] i'd say it's about a six.
[doctor] okay. and is it more painful when you stand after you've been sitting for a while?
[patient] yeah, i have to use a cane to get my bearings.
[doctor] okay. and so the injection that we gave you last time, how did that go?
[patient] it was only beneficial for a few weeks. it really helped and then it went back to the same thing.
[doctor] okay. and you're having whole right leg swelling since then?
[patient] yeah, i've been having that too. i wear support hose, and that helps a little bit, but i haven't been able to go to physical therapy.
[doctor] okay. and how is your other joints? how are you doing with your other joints?
[patient] they're doing okay. they're not as bad as my right knee.
[doctor] okay. and are you taking any other medications right now?
[patient] i'm taking xeljanz, 11 milligrams daily. and i'm taking celebrex, 200 milligrams daily.
[doctor] okay. and are you experiencing any side effects from those medications?
[patient] not that i know of.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any changes in your diet or exercise?
[patient] i don't think so. i haven't really changed anything.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any recent illnesses or fevers?
[patient] no, i've been okay.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any new symptoms? any new joint pain or anything like that?
[patient] no, just the same pain in my right knee.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any chest pain or shortness of breath?
[patient] no, no.
[doctor] okay. and any palpitations or dizziness?
[patient] no, none of that.
[doctor] okay. and any headaches or vision changes?
[patient] no, no headaches or vision changes.
[doctor] okay. and any abdominal pain or nausea?
[patient] no, no.
[doctor] okay. and any diarrhea or constipation?
[patient] no, none of that.
[doctor] okay. and any cough or hoarseness?
[patient] no, no.
[doctor] okay. and any muscle or joint pain other than your right knee?
[patient] no, just the right knee.
[doctor] okay. and any numbness or tingling?
[patient] no, no.
[doctor] okay. and any weight loss or weight gain?
[patient] no, no.
[doctor] okay. and any skin rashes or itching?
[patient] no, no.
[doctor] okay. and any ear pain or hearing loss?
[patient] no, no.
[doctor] okay. and any nasal congestion or sore throat?
[patient] no, no.
[doctor] okay. and any urinary or urinary retention?
[patient] no, no.
[doctor] okay. and any menstrual irregularities?
[patient] no, no.
[doctor] okay. and any neck pain or stiffness?
[patient] no, no.
[doctor] okay. and any back pain or stiffness?
[patient] no, no.
[doctor] okay. and any flank pain or hematuria?
[patient] no, no.
[doctor] okay. and any joint swelling or stiffness?
[patient] just my right knee.
[doctor] okay. and any muscle weakness or fatigue?
[patient] no, no.
[doctor] okay. and any general malaise or fatigue?
[patient] no, no.
[doctor] okay. and any other symptoms? any other symptoms that you're having?
[patient] no, no.
[doctor] okay. well, let's take a look at your right knee. so on physical exam of your right knee, you have swollen right knee, slightly reduced range of motion. your skin is warm to touch. you pitt with pressure. does that hurt?
[patient] yeah, that hurts.
[doctor] okay. so let's do a procedure. so we're going to do a fluid aspiration and steroid injection of your right knee. so i'm gon na explain the risks and benefits of this procedure. the risks of the steroid injection include, but are not limited to, hypopigmentation, soft tissue atrophy, tendon ruptures, and transient hyperglycemia, among others. do you have any questions about that?
[patient] no, i don't.
[doctor] okay. so let's get started. so i'm gon na access your right knee through the lateral approach. i'm gon na inject three milliliters of initially clear fluid, then blood-tinged fluid. and then i'm gon na prepped in a standard fashion, inject right knee with 80 milligrams triamcinolone and one milliliter of lidocaine. ready?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] go. okay. so the procedure was tolerated well. there were no complications. post-procedure instructions were given. so we'll have you go ahead and sit up and try to bend your knee as much as you can. okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] so for your rheumatoid arthritis, we'll continue xeljanz xr 11 milligrams daily. you will also continue celebrex 200 milligrams daily. for your high-risk medication use, you're currently on the high-risk medication xeljanz. we'll check your cmp levels and monitor periodically every couple of months. for your right knee pain, you have right knee pain and swelling with a pain level of six out of 10. on exam today, your right knee continues to be hot, which is ongoing inflammation. we performed a fluid aspiration of your right knee, and a steroid injection to your right knee, see procedure note above. we'll arrange for x-ray of your right knee and refer you to physical therapy. and for your lower extremity pitting edema, you're noting whole right leg swelling since the last visit. you usually wear support hose, which helps to some degree. you have not seen your pcp. we'll discuss compression stockings, elevate feet, and a low salt diet. you will check with your pcp for further instructions. do you have any questions?
[patient] no, i don't.
[doctor] okay. great. we'll get you checked out. }}<|eot_id|>
