dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, jacqueline. how are you?
[patient] i'm doing well. how are you?
[doctor] i'm doing well. i'm sorry i'm a little early. i know you said you wanted to come in at 8:00.
[patient] yes, i did.
[doctor] so, jacqueline, i know you were here last time for perioral dermatitis. how are you doing with that?
[patient] i'm doing okay. it's not fully resolved, but it's much better.
[doctor] okay. and i know you were using the doxycycline twice a day. how are you doing with that?
[patient] i was doing that for a little while, but my pharmacist said, you know, she said, " hey, you can cut that down to once a day. it's working."
[doctor] okay. and so you've been doing that for a week now?
[patient] yeah, for about a week.
[doctor] okay. and has it improved?
[patient] it hasn't gotten worse, but it's not fully resolved.
[doctor] okay. have you noticed any drying of the skin?
[patient] no, i haven't noticed any drying of the skin.
[doctor] okay. and you're still using the sulfacetamide wash twice a day?
[patient] yes, i am.
[doctor] okay. and are you applying the metrocream right after the sulfacetamide wash?
[patient] yes, i am.
[doctor] okay. and are you using any other lotions or products?
[patient] no, i'm not.
[doctor] okay. i know you were thinking about the elidel, but you were concerned about the side effects. have you decided to use that at all?
[patient] no, i haven't. i'm just worried about the side effects.
[doctor] okay. well, we'll talk a little bit more about that in a second. so, let's do a quick physical exam. i'm just gon na look at your skin on your face. okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] on your bilateral medial cheeks, i do see a few very faint erythematous papules with some mild redness around and underneath the nostrils. how have you been doing with your rosacea?
[patient] i've been doing okay with that.
[doctor] okay. and you're using the metrocream twice a day for that?
[patient] yes, i am.
[doctor] okay. and are you washing your eyelids gently with a cleanser?
[patient] yes, i am.
[doctor] okay. and are you using any other creams on your eyelids?
[patient] no, just the metrocream.
[doctor] okay. well, let me tell you a little bit about my assessment and plan. so, for your perioral dermatitis, i think you need to go back to using the doxycycline twice a day until we reach full resolution, and then you can go back to once a day for an additional week. i know your pharmacist said you could go to once a day, but i think we need to push through a little bit longer to get it fully resolved. how does that sound to you?
[patient] that sounds good to me.
[doctor] okay. and i don't think you should be using the elidel. i know you were concerned about the side effects, but i don't think it's necessary at this time. i would like to prescribe tacrolimus ointment. it's a very potent cream. you can apply a thin layer to the affected area twice a day. it's very expensive, but i think it will really help. are you okay with that?
[patient] yes, i'm okay with that.
[doctor] okay. and continue using the sulfacetamide wash once a day, and use the bruder mask as needed. and for your rosacea, continue using the metrocream twice daily. i would also recommend washing your eyelids gently with a cleanser once daily and trying a gentle cream such as vanicream. do you have any questions?
[patient] no, i don't.
[doctor] okay. great. and we'll see you back in four weeks. if your symptoms worsen, please call the clinic.
[patient] okay, i will.
[doctor] thank you. }}<|eot_id|>
