dialogue is: {{ [doctor] dennis martin. date of birth 01/01/1975. he's a pleasant 46 -year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right heel pain. the onset of his pain began 10 days ago while training for a triathlon. he locates his pain to the posterior aspect of his heel, which radiates proximally into his calf. his pain is aggravated by working out and when he first gets up in the morning. if he sits for a long period, his pain will return upon standing. the patient states that he runs 10 kilometers in the morning. at lunchtime, he tries to get on a bike for about 45 minutes to an hour. in the evening, he goes to the aquatic center and swims for 30 minutes to one hour. the patient states that he runs five days per week. he adds that he alternates between a long run and a long bike ride on weekends. he currently takes one day off from his exercises. the patient states that he doesn't stretch at all. he denies any left leg pain. all questions were answered. patient is very motivated to continue his triathlon training and is eager to get back to his normal activities.
[doctor] okay, dennis, let me go ahead and do a physical exam on you. are you ready?
[patient] yes.
[doctor] okay. so on your physical exam, your heart is regular, your lungs are clear to auscultation, and your abdomen is soft and nontender. on your lower extremity exam of your right heel, you do have pain with resisted extension. you do have mild pain with flexion. you do have a positive calcaneal squeeze test. you do have severe tenderness to your achilles tendon. your thompson test is negative, bilaterally. so based on your physical exam today, i do believe that you have achilles tendinitis. i would recommend that you take at least one week off from your training to allow your body to recover. i'm going to refer you to physical therapy, and they will help you with some exercises to strengthen your right lower extremities. i'm also going to prescribe you three tablets of ibuprofen every six hours for pain. if your pain does not improve with physical therapy, we will consider obtaining an x-ray. does that sound good?
[patient] yeah, that's fine. i'll take the week off and i'll try to stretch. i just don't know how, but i'll look that up.
[doctor] okay, and i'm gon na give you a handout on some exercises that you can do at home. it's important to stretch your achilles tendon before and after you run. this will help prevent this from happening in the future. do you have any other questions for me?
[patient] no, i think that's it.
[doctor] okay, great. i'll see you back in seven to 10 days to check on your progress. thank you.
[patient] thank you. }}<|eot_id|>
